+# Enabling mTLS for gRPC services with Vault PKI and CSI
+
+This document provides a step‑by‑step guide to enable mTLS for the Aegis gRPC services using HashiCorp Vault PKI and the Kubernetes Secrets Store CSI driver.
+
+Prerequisites
+- A running Vault instance with PKI enabled (or ability to enable it).
+- Vault token with permissions to create roles and issue certificates.
+- Kubernetes cluster with the Secrets Store CSI driver and the Vault provider installed.
+- `vault` CLI, `kubectl`, and `gh`/`helm` installed for operator actions.
+
+Steps
+1. Bootstrap Vault PKI (operator-run)
+   - Run (on a machine with Vault CLI and a Vault admin token):
+     ```
+     export VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.example.internal:8200
+     export VAULT_TOKEN=<vault-token>
+     bash ops/vault/pki_bootstrap.sh
+     ```
+   - This enables `pki` at path `pki`, generates a root CA (if missing), and creates a role `aegis-grpc-server`.
+
+2. Create a SecretProviderClass in your cluster
+   - Apply the example SecretProviderClass:
+     ```
+     kubectl apply -f ops/vault/secretproviderclass-full.yaml
+     ```
+   - Edit `vaultAddress` and other provider parameters to match your Vault CSI driver configuration.
+
+3. Configure your gRPC deployments to mount certs
+   - Example pod mount (see ops/grpc/k8s/evidence-deployment-mtls.yaml):
+     - Mount path: `/etc/certs`
+     - Files expected by Aegis TLS helpers:
+       - `/etc/certs/tls.crt` (certificate)
+       - `/etc/certs/tls.key` (private key)
+       - `/etc/certs/ca.crt` (optional client CA for mTLS)
+   - Apply the deployment manifest with the volume referencing the SecretProviderClass.
+
+4. Enable gRPC servers to use mTLS
+   - The gRPC servers (ops/grpc/*_server.py) detect certs at `/etc/certs` and will start secure ports when present.
+   - Ensure the `GRPC_CERT_DIR` env var (optional) points to your mount.
+
+5. Deploy a rotation job (optional)
+   - The example CronJob ops/vault/cert_rotation_job.yaml is a skeleton: adapt it to request new certs from Vault periodically and ensure CSI/driver updates the mounted files.
+   - Cert rotation frequency: choose a TTL shorter than the Vault-issued cert TTL (e.g., rotate daily for 72h certs).
+
+6. Client configuration
+   - For gRPC clients inside cluster, use `grpc.secure_channel` and load CA cert from `/etc/certs/ca.crt` (ops/grpc/tls_helpers.py provides helpers).
+   - For external clients, provision client certs via Vault and mount them as appropriate.
+
+7. Test mTLS
+   - Start the evidence gRPC server and check it binds on secure port:
+     ```
+     kubectl logs deploy/aegis-evidence-grpc -n aegis
+     ```
+   - Use the client helper with `client_credentials()` from ops/grpc/tls_helpers.py to connect via TLS.
+
+Notes & operational guidance
+- Ensure SecretProviderClass and Vault provider are configured to write certs atomically so gRPC server sees new files on rotation.
+- Treat Vault tokens and policies with least privilege: only allow CSR issuance for the role `aegis-grpc-server`.
+- Monitor cert expiration and rotation job logs; alert when certs are close to expiry.
+- Consider putting a sidecar (Vault Agent) to refresh certs and notify the main process (SIGHUP) if your server needs explicit reload.
+
